export const D6={title:'Chairside assisting and patient care',hook:'In four-handed dentistry, the dentist never looks away from the mouth to find an instrument: the assistant anticipates, transfers and evacuates. That teamwork depends on positioning, equipment knowledge and a smooth routine from seating the patient to dismissing them—the steps in HOSA Skill III.',objectives:['Identify dental office equipment and its functions.','Apply the principles of four-handed dentistry, including zones of activity and positioning.','Transfer instruments and maintain the operating field with evacuation.','Seat, prepare and dismiss a patient following HOSA Skill III.'],sections:[
['The treatment room and equipment',[
'The **dental chair** supports the patient in upright, supine or subsupine positions and has programmable controls. The **operator’s stool** supports the dentist with feet flat and thighs parallel to the floor; the **assistant’s stool** is positioned **4–6 inches higher** than the operator’s, with a footrest and an abdominal or body bar for support. The **dental unit** delivers air, water and electricity to the **handpieces** and the **air-water syringe** (three-way syringe: air, water or spray). The **rheostat** is the foot control that operates the handpieces and controls their **speed**. The **operating light** illuminates the mouth and is positioned to avoid shining into the patient’s eyes. The **high-volume evacuator (HVE)** removes large volumes of fluid and debris and reduces aerosols; the **saliva ejector** removes small amounts of fluid. Other equipment includes the **curing light**, **amalgamator** (triturator), X-ray unit and the **central vacuum and compressor**. At the start of each day, waterlines are flushed and units checked.',
match('Match the equipment to its function',[['Rheostat','Foot control that regulates handpiece speed'],['Air-water syringe','Delivers air, water or spray'],['High-volume evacuator','Removes fluids and debris and reduces aerosols'],['Saliva ejector','Removes small amounts of saliva and water'],['Curing light','Hardens light-cured materials'],['Amalgamator','Mixes (triturates) amalgam capsules']])]],
['Four-handed dentistry and positioning',[
'**Four-handed dentistry** is an ergonomic method in which a seated operator and assistant work efficiently together with the patient supine. Patient positions include **upright** (seating, dismissal and some radiographs), **supine** (lying back with head and knees at about the same level—used for most procedures) and **subsupine** (head lower than feet—used for emergencies such as syncope). Work areas are described as **zones of activity** using a clock face around the patient’s head. For a **right-handed operator**, the **operator’s zone** is about **7 to 12 o’clock**, the **transfer zone** **4 to 7 o’clock** (instruments are passed below the patient’s chin), the **assistant’s zone** **2 to 4 o’clock** and the **static zone** **12 to 2 o’clock** for equipment out of the patient’s view. For a left-handed operator the zones are reversed. The rating sheet for Skill III specifies a **12–14 inch** distance between the patient’s face and the operator’s face.',
sort('Zone for a right-handed operator',['Operator’s zone (7–12)','Transfer zone (4–7)','Assistant’s zone (2–4)','Static zone (12–2)'],[['Where the dentist sits',0],['Where instruments are passed below the chin',1],['Where the assistant sits',2],['Where the mobile cart sits out of view',3],['8 o’clock position',0],['3 o’clock position',2]])]],
['Instrument transfer and evacuation',[
'The assistant typically holds the **HVE in the right hand** and transfers instruments with the **left hand** (for a right-handed operator), anticipating the next instrument in the **order of use**. In a **single-handed exchange**, the assistant picks up the next instrument with the thumb and first two fingers, holds it parallel to the instrument in use, retrieves the used instrument with the last fingers and delivers the new one firmly into the operator’s fingers with the **working end positioned for the arch** being treated. Operators use grasps such as the **pen grasp** (most instruments), **palm grasp** (forceps and dental dam clamps) and **palm-thumb grasp** (air-water syringe). Instruments are not passed over the patient’s face. Evacuation keeps the field clear: the HVE tip is held with a **thumb-to-nose** or **pen grasp**, placed near the tooth being prepared with the bevel parallel to the tooth surface, and kept away from the soft palate to avoid gagging. Retraction of the cheek and tongue with the mirror or HVE protects tissue and improves visibility.',
chk('Where are instruments transferred during four-handed dentistry?',['In the transfer zone below the patient’s chin','Over the patient’s eyes','Behind the patient’s head','In the static zone'],'The transfer zone keeps instruments out of the patient’s view and away from the face.')]],
['Seating and dismissing a patient (HOSA Skill III)',[
'Before the patient arrives, the competitor **ensures the treatment room is cleaned and prepared**, positions the chair and clears the path, and sets up instruments and materials per the scenario. They **greet the patient courteously**, make eye contact and smile, introduce themselves, escort the patient, place personal items in a safe place and ask if the patient has questions. To **seat** the patient, they instruct them to sit on the side of the chair and swing their legs onto the base, place the chair arm, and have the patient slide up until the head is even with the top of the headrest. A **disposable napkin** is placed over the chest and clamped with a napkin chain. The competitor **informs the patient before adjusting the chair**, adjusts it **slowly**, establishes a **12–14 inch** face-to-face distance, positions the **operating light over the patient’s chest** and turns it on, reviews the preparations, performs hand hygiene and puts on PPE.',
'When the judge states that treatment is complete, the competitor positions the light out of the way, **warns the patient not to get out of the chair until it stops moving**, uses the reset button to lower the chair and return it upright, removes the napkin into the infectious waste container or biohazard bag, removes PPE, performs hand hygiene, **completes patient records**, provides any postoperative instructions, clears the path and positions the armrest, assists the patient from the chair, returns personal items and escorts the patient to the business office—using appropriate verbal and nonverbal communication throughout (46 points; 70% is 32.2).',
order('Seat the patient (HOSA Skill III)',['Prepare the room and set up instruments per the scenario','Greet, make eye contact, introduce yourself and escort the patient','Place personal items safely and ask about questions','Have the patient sit on the side, then swing legs onto the chair','Lower the chair arm and have the patient slide up to the headrest','Place and clamp the patient napkin','Tell the patient, then adjust the chair slowly to 12–14 inches','Position the light over the chest and turn it on'])]],
['Patient communication and special needs',[
'Patients may be anxious, in pain or unfamiliar with dental care. Explain procedures in plain language, use the **tell-show-do** approach with children, offer agreed hand signals for breaks and maintain privacy. Watch for **postural hypotension** when returning a patient from supine to upright—raise the chair slowly and have the patient sit for a moment before standing. Older adults may need help with mobility, hearing or vision; patients in wheelchairs may transfer to the dental chair with assistance or be treated in their chair. Pregnant patients are often more comfortable with a slight left tilt in later pregnancy. Postoperative instructions—for example, avoiding chewing until anesthesia wears off—are given verbally and in writing.',
kase('Dismissing the patient',{a:{text:'Treatment is complete. The patient reaches for the armrest as the chair begins to move upright. What should you say?',choices:[['“Please stay seated until the chair stops moving.”','b','Correct. This is a Skill III step that prevents falls.',true],['Nothing; let them stand','b','Standing while the chair moves risks injury.',false]]},b:{text:'The patient says they feel light-headed after sitting up. What now?',choices:[['Have them remain seated for a moment, then assist them from the chair','c','Correct. Postural hypotension resolves with time.',true],['Hurry them to the front desk','c','They could faint.',false]]},c:{text:'You complete the record, give postoperative instructions, return their belongings and escort them to the business office.',end:true}})]],
["Moisture control and isolation",["A dry, visible field is essential for bonding and for accurate work. **Cotton rolls** placed in the vestibule or under the tongue absorb saliva, **dry angles** (triangular pads) cover the parotid duct opening, and the **HVE** and **saliva ejector** remove fluids continuously. The **dental dam** provides the best isolation: it keeps the field dry, improves visibility, protects the patient from swallowing or inhaling debris and instruments, and reduces aerosols. To place it, the dam is **punched** with holes for the teeth to be isolated, a **clamp** with a ligature (floss tied to the bow for safety) is selected and tried on the anchor tooth, and the clamp and dam are placed with **dental dam forceps** (winged clamps may be placed with the dam), the dam is stretched over the teeth and secured with a frame, contacts are passed with floss, and the dam edges are **inverted** into the sulcus with a blunt instrument and air to seal. Removal reverses the steps: cut the interseptal dam with crown and bridge scissors, remove the clamp, frame and dam, and check that no fragments remain between teeth.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Place a dental dam","steps":["Punch holes for the teeth to be isolated","Select a clamp, tie a safety ligature and try it on the anchor tooth","Place the clamp and dam with dental dam forceps","Stretch the dam over the teeth and attach the frame","Floss the dam through the contacts","Invert the dam edges into the sulcus"]},{"t":"check","q":"Why is floss tied to a dental dam clamp?","options":["So the clamp can be retrieved if it dislodges","To make the clamp look neat","To hold the frame","To clean the teeth"],"why":"A safety ligature prevents swallowing or aspiration of the clamp."}]]
],takeaways:['The rheostat controls handpiece speed; the HVE removes fluids and reduces aerosols; the assistant sits 4–6 inches higher.','Right-handed zones: operator 7–12, transfer 4–7 (below the chin), assistant 2–4, static 12–2; supine for most care.','Skill III: prepare, greet, seat, napkin, inform before adjusting, 12–14 in, light over chest; warn the patient to stay seated until the chair stops.'],terms:[['Four-handed dentistry','Seated operator and assistant working together efficiently.'],['Transfer zone','Area below the patient’s chin where instruments are exchanged.'],['Postural hypotension','Drop in blood pressure on sitting or standing up quickly.']],mcqs:[q('In the dental unit, what does the rheostat control?','Speed of the handpiece','Air-water syringe','Chair head position','Overhead light','A Robinson sample question.'),q('For a right-handed operator, the transfer zone is:','4 to 7 o’clock','12 to 2 o’clock','7 to 12 o’clock','2 to 4 o’clock','Instruments pass below the chin.'),q('Which patient position has the head lower than the feet?','Subsupine','Upright','Supine','Semi-upright','It is used for emergencies like syncope.'),q('The assistant’s stool should be:','4–6 inches higher than the operator’s','At the same height','Lower than the operator’s','On the floor','This gives visibility.'),q('In Skill III, the face-to-face distance is:','12–14 inches','2–4 inches','24–30 inches','6 feet','It is on the rating sheet.'),q('Where is the operating light first positioned in Skill III?','Over the patient’s chest','Directly into the eyes','Behind the chair','On the floor','Then it is adjusted to the mouth.'),q('Which grasp is used for the air-water syringe?','Palm-thumb grasp','Pen grasp','Fist grasp','Modified pen only','It allows control of the buttons.'),q('Before adjusting the chair, the assistant should:','Inform the patient','Turn off the light','Remove the napkin','Ask the patient to stand','Skill III requires informing first.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how a dental assistant prepares for, seats, positions and dismisses a patient for a periodic oral evaluation following HOSA Skill III, and explain how four-handed dentistry principles apply.',rubric:[['Preparation and greeting',['clean','prepared','setup','greet','eye contact','introduce']],['Seating and positioning',['side of the chair','headrest','napkin','inform','slowly','12']],['Four-handed principles',['zone','transfer','hve','supine','stool']],['Dismissal',['warn','stops moving','records','postoperative','escort']]],model:'I would confirm the treatment room is cleaned and prepared, position the chair and clear the path, and set up instruments and materials for the evaluation. I would greet the patient courteously with eye contact and a smile, introduce myself, escort them in, place their belongings safely and ask if they have questions. I would have them sit on the side of the chair and swing their legs onto it, lower the arm, slide up to the headrest, place and clamp the napkin, tell them before adjusting the chair, recline them slowly to a supine position about 12–14 inches from the operator’s face and position the light over the chest before turning it on. Then I would perform hand hygiene and don PPE. In four-handed dentistry, the dentist works in the 7–12 o’clock zone, I sit 4–6 inches higher in the 2–4 o’clock zone and pass instruments in the transfer zone below the chin while keeping the field clear with the HVE. At dismissal, I would move the light, warn the patient to stay seated until the chair stops, reset the chair upright, remove the napkin to biohazard waste, remove PPE, perform hand hygiene, complete records, give postoperative instructions, assist the patient up, return belongings and escort them to the business office.'},sources:[RULES,DANB,ADEA]};

export const D7={title:'Dental instruments: identification and use (Skill I)',hook:'Skill I gives you 15 instruments and 15 minutes. Each one earns 2 points for the correct, correctly spelled name and 1 point for its purpose—45 points in all, and it may be given with the Round One test. Recognizing an instrument means knowing its working end, its family and the procedure it belongs to.',objectives:['Recognize the parts of a hand instrument and the major instrument families.','Name and state the purpose of every instrument on the HOSA Skill I list.','Spell instrument names exactly as listed.','Assemble instruments into trays by procedure in order of use.'],sections:[
['Parts of an instrument and tray setups',[
'A hand instrument has a **handle** (shaft), a **shank** that connects the handle to the working end and may be angled for access, and a **working end**—a blade, nib, point or beak. Instruments may be **single-** or **double-ended**. Many have numbers, such as **Black’s instrument formula** or manufacturer numbers (Cryer 150 and 151 forceps). Instruments are arranged on **preset trays** or cassettes **in order of use**, from left to right: basic setup first (**mouth mirror**, **explorer**, **cotton forceps**), then instruments for the procedure. Knowing tray setups helps identify an instrument by its role.',
{t:'table',caption:'Skill I scoring',head:['Element','Points'],rows:[['Correct name and spelling (each of 15 instruments)','2 (1 name + 1 spelling)'],['Correct purpose or use (each)','1'],['Total for 15 instruments','45 (30 name/spelling + 15 purpose)']]}]],
['Examination, hand cutting and restorative instruments',[
'The **endodontic explorer** has long, straight, sharp tips used to **locate root canal openings (orifices)**. The **spoon excavator** removes **soft carious dentin**, debris and temporary restorations. **Amalgam instruments** include the **amalgam well** (holds triturated amalgam), the **double-ended amalgam carrier** (carries and dispenses amalgam into the preparation) and the **amalgam condenser** (packs amalgam firmly into the preparation). **Burnishers**—**ball**, **football**, **T-ball** and **acorn**—smooth and polish the surface of an amalgam restoration and help contour matrix bands. **Carvers** shape the anatomy of amalgam: the **discoid-cleoid** carves **occlusal** anatomy, and the **Hollenback** carves **interproximal and proximal** surfaces. The **composite placement instrument** (often plastic or coated so it does not stick) places and shapes composite resin. The **liner applicator** (ball-ended) places liners such as calcium hydroxide. The **articulating paper holder** holds articulating paper to **mark occlusal contacts** so high spots can be adjusted.',
match('Match the restorative instrument to its purpose',[['Amalgam carrier','Carries and dispenses amalgam into the preparation'],['Amalgam condenser','Packs amalgam into the preparation'],['Discoid-cleoid carver','Carves occlusal anatomy'],['Hollenback carver','Carves interproximal surfaces'],['Ball burnisher','Smooths and polishes amalgam'],['Spoon excavator','Removes soft carious dentin and debris'],['Liner applicator','Places liners such as calcium hydroxide'],['Articulating paper holder','Marks occlusal high spots']])]],
['Accessory, isolation and matrix instruments',[
'The **universal matrix band** with its **retainer (Tofflemire)** replaces the missing **proximal wall** during a Class II restoration so material can be condensed and contoured. **Howe (110) pliers** carry cotton products and place or remove matrix bands and wedges. **Bird beak pliers** bend and contour **wires and clasps** in orthodontic and prosthetic work. **Crown and bridge scissors** trim matrix bands, temporary crowns and retraction cord; **suture scissors** cut sutures. **Dental dam** instruments isolate teeth: the **dental dam punch** makes holes in the dam, **dental dam forceps** place and remove the clamp, and **universal clamps** for maxillary and mandibular molars come **winged** (wings hold the dam so clamp and dam can be placed together) or **wingless** (placed before the dam). **Bite-wing tabs** attach to film or sensors so the patient bites to hold them for **bitewing radiographs**. The **tongue and cheek retractor** holds tissue away for access and visibility.',
sort('Instrument family',['Restorative (amalgam/composite)','Isolation and matrix','Surgical'],[['Amalgam condenser',0],['Discoid-cleoid carver',0],['Dental dam punch',1],['Universal matrix band',1],['Winged mandibular clamp',1],['Root-tip elevator',2],['Rongeurs',2],['Cryer 150 forceps',2],['Composite placement instrument',0]])]],
['Surgical and periodontal instruments',[
'**Elevators** loosen teeth before extraction: the **straight elevator** severs the periodontal ligament and luxates the tooth, and the **root-tip elevator** removes small root tips and fragments. **Extraction forceps** grasp and remove teeth: the **Cryer 150** is the **maxillary universal** forceps and the **Cryer 151** the **mandibular universal** forceps. **Rongeurs** trim and contour **alveolar bone**, and the **bone file** smooths rough bone edges. The **hemostat** clamps vessels, grasps tissue and retrieves small objects. **Sickle scalers** remove **supragingival calculus**—the **straight sickle** is used mainly on anterior teeth and the **curved sickle** on posterior teeth and proximal surfaces.']],
['Handpieces, burs and powered equipment',[
'The **high-speed handpiece** (and **fiberoptic high-speed handpiece**, which adds light at the head) removes tooth structure quickly, prepares teeth and removes old restorations, using friction-grip burs with water spray to cool the tooth. The **slow-speed handpiece with a contra-angle attachment** removes caries and refines preparations with latch-type burs; with a **prophy angle attachment** it holds a rubber cup or brush for **coronal polishing**. Burs have specific shapes: **round** burs open the tooth and remove caries; **inverted cone** burs create undercuts for retention; **pear-shaped** burs remove caries and shape preparations; **finishing** burs (many fluted blades) contour and smooth restorations; **diamond** burs (abrasive particles) reduce tooth structure for crowns and smooth enamel. The **high-volume evacuation tip** removes fluids and debris and retracts tissue, the **curing light** polymerizes light-cured materials, and the **anesthetic aspirating syringe** delivers local anesthetic while allowing aspiration.',
match('Match the bur to its use',[['Round bur','Opens the tooth and removes decay'],['Inverted cone bur','Creates undercuts for retention'],['Pear-shaped bur','Removes caries and shapes preparations'],['Finishing bur','Contours and smooths restorations'],['Diamond bur','Rapid reduction for crown preparations'],['Prophy angle with rubber cup','Coronal polishing']]),
{t:'w',kind:'spellinglab',title:'Spell the instrument names',prompt:'Read the purpose, then type the instrument name. Skill I awards a point for correct spelling of each name.',note:'Spelling practice for HOSA Skill I. Device speech is optional and may mispronounce terms; follow the spellings in the HOSA instrument list.',words:[['Hollenback','Carver used for interproximal and proximal surfaces of amalgam'],['discoid-cleoid','Carver for occlusal anatomy'],['burnisher','Instrument that smooths and polishes amalgam'],['excavator','Spoon-shaped instrument that removes soft carious dentin'],['rongeurs','Surgical instrument that trims alveolar bone'],['hemostat','Clamp used to grasp tissue or small objects'],['elevator','Instrument that loosens a tooth before extraction'],['condenser','Instrument that packs amalgam into a preparation'],['articulating','Type of paper used to mark occlusal contacts'],['retractor','Instrument that holds the tongue and cheek away']]}]],
['Practicing for Skill I',[
'Study the official list as a set of flashcards by family, practice with real instruments when possible and say each purpose in one clear sentence beginning with a verb (“packs amalgam into the preparation”). Learn the distinguishing features: number and shape of nibs, curved versus straight shanks, smooth versus serrated beaks, single versus double ends. Spell names exactly as on the list, including hyphens (discoid-cleoid) and numbers (Cryer 150, Cryer 151). Time yourself with 15 instruments in 15 minutes.',
flip('Quick identification cues',[['Cryer 150 vs 151','150 maxillary universal; 151 mandibular universal'],['Winged vs wingless clamp','Winged: placed with the dam; wingless: placed before the dam'],['Straight vs curved sickle scaler','Straight mainly anterior; curved posterior and proximal'],['Discoid-cleoid vs Hollenback','Occlusal carving vs interproximal carving'],['Contra-angle vs prophy angle','Caries removal with burs vs coronal polishing'],['Endodontic explorer','Long, straight sharp tips to find canal orifices']])]]
],takeaways:['Skill I: 15 instruments, 2 points for name and spelling plus 1 for purpose (45 points); it may be given with the test.','Restorative: carrier, condenser, burnishers, discoid-cleoid (occlusal) and Hollenback (interproximal) carvers; spoon excavator removes soft caries.','Surgical: elevators, Cryer 150 (maxillary) and 151 (mandibular) forceps, rongeurs, bone file; burs: round, inverted cone, pear, finishing, diamond.'],terms:[['Working end','Part of an instrument that performs its function.'],['Shank','Part connecting handle and working end.'],['Matrix band','Thin metal strip that forms a temporary proximal wall.']],mcqs:[q('Which instrument carves occlusal anatomy of amalgam?','Discoid-cleoid carver','Hollenback carver','Spoon excavator','Amalgam carrier','The Hollenback carves interproximal areas.'),q('The Cryer 151 forceps are designed for:','Mandibular teeth','Maxillary teeth','Primary teeth only','Implants','Cryer 150 is maxillary.'),q('Which instrument is best for examining teeth for caries and calculus?','Explorer','Dull chisel','Ball burnisher','Scalpel','A Boyd sample question.'),q('Rongeurs are used to:','Trim and contour alveolar bone','Place composite','Mark occlusion','Polish teeth','They are surgical instruments.'),q('Which bur creates undercuts for retention?','Inverted cone','Round','Finishing','Diamond','Its shape allows undercutting.'),q('A winged dental dam clamp:','Can be placed together with the dam','Is never used with a dam','Is only for anterior teeth','Is placed after the dam','Wings hold the dam.'),q('The spoon excavator removes:','Soft carious dentin and debris','Calculus below the gumline','Bone','Sutures','It is a hand cutting instrument.'),q('How many points are possible in Skill I?','45','30','15','60','30 for names and spelling, 15 for purposes.')],frq:{prompt:'List the instruments you would place on a tray for a Class II amalgam restoration in order of use, and state the purpose of each.',rubric:[['Basic setup',['mirror','explorer','cotton forceps']],['Preparation instruments',['handpiece','bur','spoon excavator','hve']],['Matrix and amalgam instruments',['matrix','retainer','carrier','condenser','well']],['Finishing instruments',['burnisher','discoid-cleoid','hollenback','articulating']]],model:'From left to right: the mouth mirror for indirect vision and retraction, the explorer for examining the tooth, and cotton forceps for transferring small items; the anesthetic aspirating syringe for local anesthesia; the high-speed handpiece with burs—such as a pear-shaped bur to prepare the tooth and remove caries—and the slow-speed contra-angle handpiece with a round bur for remaining decay, with the HVE tip to remove fluids; a spoon excavator for soft carious dentin; the liner applicator if a liner is needed; the universal matrix band and retainer to recreate the missing proximal wall, placed with Howe pliers and a wedge; the amalgam well holding triturated amalgam, the amalgam carrier to dispense it and the amalgam condenser to pack it; burnishers to smooth it; the discoid-cleoid carver for occlusal anatomy and the Hollenback carver for the interproximal area; and the articulating paper holder with paper to check and adjust the occlusion.'},sources:[RULES,ADEA,DANB]};

export const D8={title:'Local anesthesia and the aspirating syringe (Skill II)',hook:'Comfortable dentistry depends on effective, safe local anesthesia. The assistant prepares the aspirating syringe, checks the anesthetic cartridge, passes the syringe safely and handles the needle so no one is injured—each step scored in HOSA Skill II.',objectives:['Explain how local anesthetics and vasoconstrictors work.','Identify the parts of the aspirating syringe, needles and cartridges.','Assemble and disassemble the aspirating syringe following HOSA Skill II.','Prevent needlestick injuries and recognize anesthetic complications.'],sections:[
['How local anesthesia works',[
'**Local anesthetics** block nerve impulses so the patient feels no pain in a specific area while remaining conscious. Most dental anesthetics are **amides**, such as **lidocaine**, **mepivacaine**, **articaine**, **prilocaine** and **bupivacaine** (longer-acting). A **vasoconstrictor**, usually **epinephrine**, narrows blood vessels to **prolong anesthesia**, **reduce bleeding** and **slow absorption into the bloodstream**, which lowers toxicity; patients with certain heart conditions may need limited epinephrine. **Topical anesthetic** (often benzocaine gel) is applied to dried mucosa before the injection to reduce needle discomfort. **Infiltration** deposits anesthetic near the apex of a tooth and works well for maxillary teeth; a **nerve block** deposits anesthetic near a nerve trunk, such as the **inferior alveolar nerve block** for mandibular teeth. **Nitrous oxide–oxygen sedation** reduces anxiety; at the end, the patient receives **100% oxygen** for several minutes to prevent diffusion hypoxia.',
match('Match the term to its meaning',[['Vasoconstrictor','Narrows vessels to prolong anesthesia'],['Topical anesthetic','Numbs the surface tissue before injection'],['Infiltration','Anesthetic placed near the root apex'],['Nerve block','Anesthetic placed near a main nerve trunk'],['Aspiration','Checking that the needle is not in a blood vessel'],['Paresthesia','Prolonged numbness or tingling after anesthesia']])]],
['The syringe, needle and cartridge',[
'The **aspirating syringe** has a **thumb ring**, **finger grip** and **finger bar**, a **barrel** with a window to view the cartridge, a **piston rod** ending in a **harpoon** that engages the rubber plunger, and a **needle adaptor** where the needle screws on. The harpoon lets the operator pull back (**aspirate**); if blood appears in the cartridge, the needle is in a vessel and must be repositioned. **Needles** are disposable and sized by **gauge**—the larger the number, the thinner the needle (25, 27 and 30 gauge are common)—and length: **long** needles for nerve blocks and **short** needles for infiltration. The **cartridge (carpule)** is a glass cylinder of about **1.8 mL** with a **rubber plunger** at one end and an **aluminum cap** with a **rubber diaphragm** at the other, which the needle pierces. Cartridges are color-coded by the ADA system (for example, a **red** band identifies 2% lidocaine with 1:100,000 epinephrine) and are **never reused** between patients.',
sort('Part of the syringe or the cartridge?',['Syringe','Cartridge'],[['Thumb ring',0],['Harpoon',0],['Needle adaptor',0],['Rubber plunger',1],['Aluminum cap and diaphragm',1],['Glass cylinder with solution',1]])]],
['Checking the cartridge',[
'Before assembly, the assistant inspects the cartridge as Skill II requires: the **color of the solution and presence of any bubbles** (solution should be clear; a large bubble may indicate freezing), the **expiration date**, the **condition of the aluminum cap and the glass** (no cracks or chips, cap not corroded) and the **position of the plunger** (an extruded plunger may indicate freezing; a sunken plunger may indicate soaking in disinfectant). Damaged, cloudy or expired cartridges are discarded. Cartridges are stored at room temperature away from light and are never autoclaved.',
chk('A cartridge’s rubber plunger is extruded beyond the glass rim. What should the assistant do?',['Discard it; it may have been frozen','Use it after warming','Push the plunger back in','Use it only for children'],'Extruded plungers suggest the cartridge was frozen.')]],
['Assembling and disassembling (HOSA Skill II)',[
'In Skill II, the competitor performs hand hygiene, dons **gown, surgical mask, protective eyewear and gloves**, obtains the **disposable needle and the anesthetic specified by the scenario** and checks the cartridge (solution color and bubbles, expiration, aluminum cap and glass, plunger position). They check the syringe and the condition of the **harpoon**, place the fingers and thumb on the **thumb ring and finger grip**, **retract the piston all the way back**, place the cartridge with the **plunger end toward the harpoon** (the aluminum cap end falls into place), and **engage the harpoon** into the rubber plunger with **moderate pressure** on the thumb ring until it is firmly engaged. To place the needle, they **break the seal** and remove the protective guard and **screw the needle onto the needle adaptor**.',
'After the judge states the procedure is complete, the competitor **carefully removes the used needle** and **discards it in the sharps container**, uses **one hand to disengage the harpoon** (pulling back on the thumb ring) and **inverts the syringe to remove the cartridge**. They remove examination gloves without contamination, put on **utility gloves**, clean and disinfect the work area, wash the utility gloves with soap and water and disinfect them with surface disinfectant, remove PPE into the regular trash and perform hand hygiene (42 points; 70% is 29.4).',
order('Assemble the aspirating syringe (HOSA Skill II)',['Hand hygiene and PPE: gown, mask, eyewear, gloves','Obtain the needle and the scenario’s anesthetic','Check the cartridge and the syringe harpoon','Retract the piston fully using the thumb ring','Insert the cartridge, plunger end toward the harpoon','Engage the harpoon with moderate pressure','Break the needle seal and remove the guard','Screw the needle onto the needle adaptor'])]],
['Safety and complications',[
'The syringe is passed in the **transfer zone below the patient’s chin**, out of the patient’s line of sight, with the needle cap loosened and the thumb ring placed over the operator’s thumb. Used needles are **recapped only with a one-handed scoop technique or a recapping device**, never with two hands, and disposed of in a sharps container as required by the OSHA Bloodborne Pathogens Standard. Watch the patient during and after injection: **anxiety and fainting** (syncope) are the most common reactions; **epinephrine effects** can cause a racing heart and nervousness; **toxicity** from too much anesthetic can cause confusion or seizures; true **allergy** to amide anesthetics is rare; and a **hematoma** (swelling from a nicked vessel) or prolonged **paresthesia** may occur. Patients are told to avoid chewing or hot drinks until numbness wears off to prevent biting the lip, tongue or cheek—especially children.',
kase('After an inferior alveolar block',{a:{text:'A child leaves the appointment with a numb lower lip. What instruction is most important?',choices:[['Avoid chewing, biting the lip or hot foods until numbness wears off','b','Correct. Children often bite numb lips and cheeks.',true],['Rinse vigorously','b','This does not prevent lip-biting injuries.',false]]},b:{text:'The needle used for the injection is uncapped on the tray. How should it be handled?',choices:[['Recap with a one-handed scoop or device, then remove and discard in the sharps container','c','Correct. Never use two hands.',true],['Recap with two hands quickly','c','Two-handed recapping causes needlesticks.',false]]},c:{text:'The needle goes into the sharps container, the cartridge is discarded and the syringe is processed for sterilization.',end:true}})]],
["Which teeth does each injection numb?",["Dental assistants prepare the correct syringe and needle and anticipate the operator’s needs based on the planned injection. In the **mandible**, the dense bone means a **nerve block** is usually required. The **inferior alveolar nerve block** numbs all mandibular teeth on one side to the midline, the lower lip and chin (mental nerve) and, with the **lingual nerve**, the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and lingual gingiva; the **long buccal block** adds the buccal gingiva of the molars; the **mental or incisive block** numbs premolars and anterior teeth. In the **maxilla**, the thinner, porous bone allows **infiltration** (supraperiosteal injection) for individual teeth, and blocks such as the **posterior superior alveolar (PSA)** block (maxillary molars), **middle superior alveolar** block (premolars), **anterior superior alveolar** block (canine and incisors), **greater palatine** block (posterior palate) and **nasopalatine** block (anterior palate) cover larger areas. Long needles are used for the inferior alveolar block; short needles for most infiltrations.",{"t":"table","caption":"Common dental injections","head":["Injection","Area anesthetized"],"rows":[["Inferior alveolar nerve block","Mandibular teeth on one side, lower lip and chin; tongue with lingual nerve"],["Long buccal block","Buccal gingiva of mandibular molars"],["Posterior superior alveolar block","Most maxillary molars and their buccal tissue"],["Nasopalatine block","Anterior hard palate"],["Greater palatine block","Posterior hard palate"],["Infiltration","Individual maxillary teeth and surrounding tissue"]]},{"t":"check","q":"Which injection is used to numb all mandibular teeth on one side?","options":["Inferior alveolar nerve block","Nasopalatine block","Posterior superior alveolar block","Greater palatine block"],"why":"The inferior alveolar nerve supplies the mandibular teeth."}]]
],takeaways:['Amide anesthetics with epinephrine prolong numbness and reduce bleeding; infiltration for maxillary teeth, blocks for mandibular.','Check the cartridge: solution and bubbles, expiration, cap and glass, plunger position; larger gauge numbers are thinner needles.','Skill II: retract piston, insert cartridge plunger-first, engage harpoon, attach needle; remove needle to sharps, disengage harpoon with one hand, invert to remove the cartridge.'],terms:[['Harpoon','Barbed tip that engages the cartridge plunger for aspiration.'],['Gauge','Needle diameter; higher numbers are thinner.'],['Cartridge (carpule)','Glass cylinder of anesthetic solution.']],mcqs:[q('Why is epinephrine added to local anesthetics?','To prolong anesthesia and reduce bleeding','To speed absorption','To cause sedation','To improve taste','It is a vasoconstrictor.'),q('In Skill II, the cartridge is inserted with:','The plunger end toward the harpoon','The aluminum cap toward the harpoon','The needle already attached','The piston fully forward','Then the harpoon is engaged.'),q('Which needle is thinnest?','30 gauge','25 gauge','27 gauge','18 gauge','Higher gauge numbers are thinner.'),q('What does aspiration check for?','Whether the needle is in a blood vessel','The cartridge expiration date','The patient’s blood pressure','Anesthetic color','Blood in the cartridge means reposition.'),q('How is the cartridge removed in Skill II?','Disengage the harpoon and invert the syringe','Pull it out with pliers','Break the glass','Leave it in for the next patient','Cartridges are single-use.'),q('Used needles are recapped:','With a one-handed scoop or recapping device','With two hands','Never removed','By the patient','This prevents needlesticks.'),q('A nerve block is most often used for:','Mandibular teeth','Maxillary central incisors only','Gingiva only','Lips only','The inferior alveolar block numbs half the mandible.'),q('A cartridge with a large bubble and extruded plunger was likely:','Frozen','Expired yesterday','Overheated','Normal','Discard it.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to safely prepare, pass and disassemble an aspirating anesthetic syringe following HOSA Skill II, including the cartridge checks and needlestick prevention.',rubric:[['PPE and cartridge checks',['gown','mask','eyewear','gloves','bubble','expiration','cap','plunger']],['Assembly steps',['retract','piston','plunger end','harpoon','moderate pressure','needle adaptor']],['Passing and needle safety',['transfer zone','below the chin','one-handed','scoop','sharps']],['Disassembly and cleanup',['remove the needle','disengage','invert','utility gloves','disinfect','hand hygiene']]],model:'I would perform hand hygiene and put on a gown, surgical mask, protective eyewear and gloves, then obtain the disposable needle and the anesthetic named in the scenario. I would check the cartridge for clear solution without large bubbles, the expiration date, an intact aluminum cap and uncracked glass, and a normally positioned plunger, and check the syringe’s harpoon. Holding the thumb ring and finger grip, I would retract the piston fully, insert the cartridge plunger end toward the harpoon, engage the harpoon with moderate pressure, break the needle seal, remove the guard and screw the needle onto the adaptor. The syringe is passed in the transfer zone below the patient’s chin, out of sight. Afterward, I would recap with a one-handed scoop or device, remove the needle and discard it in the sharps container, disengage the harpoon with one hand, invert the syringe to remove the cartridge, remove gloves without contamination, put on utility gloves, clean and disinfect the area, wash and disinfect the utility gloves, remove PPE and perform hand hygiene.'},sources:[RULES,OSHA_BBP,MEDLINE_ANES]};

export const D9={title:'Restorative and laboratory materials; alginate impressions (Skill VI)',hook:'Every restoration is a materials decision: amalgam for a stress-bearing molar, composite for a front tooth, a sedative cement under a temporary crown. The assistant mixes and delivers these materials with precise timing—and Skill VI tests the most common one, alginate.',objectives:['Compare amalgam, composite, glass ionomer and other restorative materials.','Describe liners, bases, cements and their uses.','Explain impression materials and gypsum products.','Prepare, mix, deliver and handle an alginate impression following HOSA Skill VI.'],sections:[
['Direct restorative materials',[
'**Amalgam** is an alloy of silver, tin, copper and sometimes zinc mixed with **mercury**. It is strong, long-lasting and economical for posterior teeth but is silver-colored and held by **mechanical retention** (undercuts). It is supplied in **precapsulated** form and mixed (**triturated**) in an **amalgamator**; mercury hygiene and **amalgam separators** protect staff and the environment. **Composite resin** is tooth-colored, made of a resin matrix, inorganic fillers and a coupling agent. It **bonds** to tooth structure after **etching** (often with 37% phosphoric acid) and a bonding agent, and most composites are **light-cured** with the curing light. **Shade selection** is done with natural light **before** isolation, because teeth lighten as they dry. **Glass ionomer** bonds chemically to tooth structure and **releases fluoride**, useful for Class V lesions and patients at high caries risk. **Temporary restorations** use materials such as reinforced zinc oxide–eugenol (IRM).',
sort('Amalgam or composite?',['Amalgam','Composite resin'],[['Contains mercury',0],['Tooth-colored',1],['Light-cured',1],['Mechanical retention with undercuts',0],['Requires etching and bonding',1],['Triturated in an amalgamator',0]])]],
['Liners, bases and cements',[
'**Liners** such as **calcium hydroxide** are placed in thin layers in deep preparations to protect the pulp and stimulate **reparative dentin**. **Bases** are thicker layers that provide thermal insulation and replace lost dentin. **Varnish** seals dentinal tubules under amalgam (not under composite). **Dental cements** are used for **luting** (permanently cementing crowns, bridges and orthodontic bands), as temporary cements, as bases and as restorations. Examples include **zinc phosphate** (mixed on a cool glass slab in small increments to control heat), **zinc oxide–eugenol (ZOE)**—soothing to the pulp but **not used under composite** because eugenol interferes with resin setting—**polycarboxylate**, **glass ionomer** and **resin cements**. Mixing times, working times and setting times are critical, and materials are dispensed only just before use.',
match('Match the material to its use',[['Calcium hydroxide liner','Protects the pulp in deep preparations'],['Zinc oxide–eugenol','Sedative temporary cement (not under composite)'],['Zinc phosphate cement','Permanent luting cement mixed on a cool slab'],['Glass ionomer','Releases fluoride; bonds chemically'],['Etchant (phosphoric acid)','Prepares enamel for bonding'],['Bonding agent','Adheres composite to tooth structure']])]],
['Impressions and gypsum products',[
'An **impression** is a negative reproduction of the teeth and tissues. **Preliminary impressions** for study models, mouthguards, bleaching trays and custom trays use **alginate**, an **irreversible hydrocolloid** that cannot return to its original state. Alginate is affected by water: it absorbs water and swells (**imbibition**) or loses water and shrinks (**syneresis**), so impressions are disinfected, kept moist and **poured promptly** (usually within an hour). **Warm water speeds setting** and cool water slows it. **Final impressions** for crowns and bridges use **elastomeric** materials such as **polyvinyl siloxane**, **polyether** and **polysulfide**, which are more accurate and stable. **Gypsum products** create models from impressions: **model plaster** for study models, **dental stone** for working casts and **high-strength (die) stone** for dies. They are mixed with a measured water-to-powder ratio and vibrated into the impression to remove bubbles. Laboratory items include **waxes** (for bite registrations and patterns), **custom trays** and **temporary crowns**.',
chk('An alginate impression is left on the counter for two hours before pouring. What happens?',['It shrinks from syneresis, causing an inaccurate model','It becomes more accurate','It turns into stone','Nothing changes'],'Pour promptly and keep the impression moist.')]],
['Alginate impressions (HOSA Skill VI)',[
'In Skill VI, the competitor performs hand hygiene, dons a **gown, surgical mask, protective eyewear and gloves** and assembles materials. They measure **room-temperature water** for the identified impression into the bowl, **fluff the powder** by rolling the container side to side several times if the manufacturer directs, and **open the container carefully to avoid inhaling the powder**. They verbalize the measures: **mandibular—2 measures of water and 2 scoops of alginate; maxillary—3 measures of water and 3 scoops**. The powder is added to the water and mixed: a **circular motion** pressing the spatula against the side of the bowl to wet all the powder, then a **stropping action** against the side of the bowl while **rotating the bowl**, until the mix is **smooth and creamy without bubbles**. Mixing time is **1 minute for regular-set** and **30–45 seconds for fast-set** alginate (both verbalized).',
'The competitor verbalizes using a tray **adhesive or lubricant spray** if recommended, **fills the tray from the back toward the front (anterior)**, smooths the surface with a **wet finger or wet spatula**, removes excess from the back of the tray and **hands the tray to the doctor handle first**. When the impression is returned, they **rinse it gently under tap water for at least 30 seconds**, **spray it with disinfectant**, **wrap it in an airtight container or moist paper towel**, place it in a **plastic bag labeled with the patient’s name**, dispose of leftover alginate in the trash, clean the bowl and place it for disinfecting, remove PPE and perform hand hygiene (33 points; 70% is 23.1).',
order('Mix and load alginate (HOSA Skill VI)',['Measure room-temperature water into the bowl','Fluff the powder and open the container carefully','Add the measured scoops of powder to the water','Mix with a circular motion against the bowl to wet the powder','Strop against the side while rotating the bowl','Mix 1 minute (regular) or 30–45 seconds (fast set)','Fill the tray from back to front and smooth with a wet finger','Hand the tray to the doctor handle first']),
kase('Timing problems',{a:{text:'Your alginate set in the bowl before you could load the tray. What is the most likely cause?',choices:[['The water was too warm or you used fast-set alginate and mixed too long','b','Correct. Warm water and long mixing shorten working time.',true],['The water was too cold','b','Cold water slows setting.',false]]},b:{text:'For the next mix, the mandibular impression needs how much alginate?',choices:[['2 scoops of powder and 2 measures of room-temperature water','c','Correct. Maxillary uses 3 and 3.',true],['3 scoops and 3 measures','c','That is for the maxillary arch.',false]]},c:{text:'The new mix is smooth and creamy, loaded back to front and handed to the doctor handle first.',end:true}})]],
["Dental laboratory procedures",["Alginate impressions become **diagnostic casts (study models)** in the laboratory. The impression is disinfected and poured in **model plaster or dental stone** measured by weight and water volume, mixed until smooth and **vibrated** into the impression from one end so the material flows into each tooth without trapping bubbles. The **anatomic portion** is poured first, and the **art portion (base)** is added to form a stable base. After the gypsum reaches its initial set (often 45–60 minutes), the cast is separated from the tray and later **trimmed** on a model trimmer so the bases are parallel to the occlusal plane, with symmetrical angles and an accurate bite when the maxillary and mandibular casts are put together with a **wax bite registration**. Casts are used for case presentations, orthodontic records, custom trays, bleaching trays, mouthguards and provisional crowns.","Other laboratory tasks include fabricating **custom impression trays** from acrylic or light-cured resin, making **provisional (temporary) crowns** from preformed shells or custom material, and preparing **laboratory prescriptions** that the dentist signs for crowns, bridges and dentures. Items sent to or returned from a commercial laboratory are **disinfected** and labeled to prevent cross-contamination. Laboratory safety includes eye protection on lathes and model trimmers, ventilation for acrylic monomer and careful handling of Bunsen burners and sharp instruments.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Pour and trim a study model","steps":["Rinse and disinfect the impression","Measure water and gypsum and mix smoothly","Vibrate the gypsum into the impression from one end","Form the base (art portion)","Separate the cast after it sets","Trim the base parallel to the occlusal plane"]}]],
["Walking through a Class II composite",["Materials and instruments come together in a typical **Class II composite restoration**. The assistant reviews the medical history, seats the patient and prepares a tray in order of use. After topical and local anesthesia, the **shade is selected in natural light** before isolation. The tooth is isolated with a **dental dam** or cotton rolls and the HVE. The dentist prepares the tooth with the high-speed handpiece and burs, removes remaining caries with a slow-speed round bur or spoon excavator and may place a **liner** if the preparation is deep. A **matrix band and wedge** recreate the proximal wall. The assistant hands the **etchant**, which is rinsed after the recommended time, then the **bonding agent**, which is air-thinned and **light-cured**. Composite is placed in **increments** with the composite placement instrument, each layer light-cured. After the matrix is removed, the restoration is shaped with **finishing burs**, strips and discs; the occlusion is checked with **articulating paper** and adjusted, and the restoration is polished. The assistant documents the materials used and gives postoperative instructions.",{"t":"example","title":"Sequence the materials","prompt":"Put these items in the order they are used for a Class II composite: bonding agent, articulating paper, etchant, matrix band and wedge, composite and curing light, shade guide.","steps":["Shade guide before isolation, while the teeth are moist.","Matrix band and wedge after the preparation is complete.","Etchant, rinsed after the recommended time.","Bonding agent, air-thinned and light-cured.","Composite placed in increments and light-cured.","Articulating paper to check the occlusion after finishing."],"answer":"Shade guide → matrix band and wedge → etchant → bonding agent → composite with curing light → articulating paper."}]]
],takeaways:['Amalgam: mercury alloy, mechanical retention, triturated; composite: tooth-colored, etched, bonded and light-cured; glass ionomer releases fluoride.','Calcium hydroxide liners protect the pulp; ZOE is sedative but not used under composite.','Skill VI: mandibular 2/2, maxillary 3/3 room-temperature water; mix 1 min regular or 30–45 s fast; load back to front; rinse 30 s, disinfect, wrap moist, bag and label.'],terms:[['Irreversible hydrocolloid','Alginate impression material that cannot return to a liquid state.'],['Syneresis','Water loss and shrinkage of an alginate impression.'],['Trituration','Mixing of amalgam alloy and mercury.']],mcqs:[q('How many scoops of alginate are used for a maxillary impression in Skill VI?','3 scoops with 3 measures of water','2 scoops with 2 measures','1 scoop with 2 measures','4 scoops with 3 measures','Mandibular uses 2 and 2.'),q('How long is regular-set alginate mixed?','1 minute','5 minutes','10 seconds','3 minutes','Fast set is 30–45 seconds.'),q('How is the tray filled?','From the back toward the anterior','From the front to the back','In the center only','Randomly','This reduces trapped air.'),q('Warm water causes alginate to:','Set faster','Set slower','Never set','Change color','Use room-temperature water.'),q('Which cement should NOT be placed under composite?','Zinc oxide–eugenol','Glass ionomer','Resin cement','Calcium hydroxide','Eugenol inhibits resin polymerization.'),q('After removal, the alginate impression is rinsed for at least:','30 seconds','5 seconds','5 minutes','It is not rinsed','Then it is disinfected.'),q('Composite shade selection is done:','Before isolation, in natural light','After the tooth is dried','After curing','In the dark','Dry teeth lighten.'),q('Which material releases fluoride?','Glass ionomer','Amalgam','Zinc phosphate','Wax','It helps prevent recurrent caries.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how to prepare, mix, deliver and handle a mandibular alginate impression following HOSA Skill VI, and explain the properties of alginate that make timing and handling important.',rubric:[['PPE and measuring',['gown','mask','eyewear','gloves','2 scoops','2 measures','room temperature']],['Mixing technique and time',['circular','strop','rotate','smooth','creamy','1 minute','30']],['Loading and delivery',['back','anterior','wet finger','excess','handle first']],['Handling and properties',['rinse','30 seconds','disinfect','moist','label','syneresis','imbibition','pour']]],model:'After hand hygiene, I would put on a gown, surgical mask, protective eyewear and gloves and assemble materials. For a mandibular impression, I would measure 2 measures of room-temperature water into the bowl, fluff the powder if the manufacturer directs, open the container carefully to avoid inhaling dust and add 2 scoops. I would mix with a circular motion pressing against the bowl to wet the powder, then strop the material against the side while rotating the bowl until it is smooth and creamy without bubbles—about 1 minute for regular set or 30–45 seconds for fast set. After applying tray adhesive if recommended, I would load the tray from back to front, smooth the surface with a wet finger, remove excess from the back and hand it to the doctor handle first. When it is returned, I would rinse it gently under tap water for at least 30 seconds, spray it with disinfectant, wrap it in a moist paper towel or airtight container, bag and label it with the patient’s name and dispose of leftover alginate. Alginate is an irreversible hydrocolloid: warm water speeds setting, and it shrinks by syneresis if it dries or swells by imbibition if soaked, so it must be kept moist and poured promptly for an accurate model.'},sources:[RULES,FDA_AMALGAM,ADEA]};

export const D10={title:'Dental radiology and medical emergencies',hook:'Radiographs reveal what the eye cannot see—decay between teeth, bone loss and abscesses—but they use ionizing radiation that must be kept as low as reasonably achievable. And while emergencies are rare in dental offices, a team that is prepared can save a life. Each topic is 10% of the test.',objectives:['Explain how dental X-rays are produced and how radiation exposure is minimized.','Compare radiographic types and techniques and recognize common errors.','Distinguish radiolucent and radiopaque structures on a bitewing.','Recognize and respond to common medical emergencies in the dental office.'],sections:[
['How X-rays are produced and controlled',[
'Dental X-rays are produced in the **tube head**. A heated **tungsten filament** on the **cathode** releases electrons, which accelerate toward the **tungsten target** on the **anode**; when they strike it, X-ray photons are produced (most of the energy becomes heat). The **position-indicating device (PID)** aims the beam, and **collimation** (round or, better, **rectangular**) restricts its size, while **aluminum filtration** removes weak, useless low-energy rays. The operator controls three factors: **kilovoltage peak (kVp)**, which controls the **penetrating power** (quality) of the beam and image **contrast**; **milliamperage (mA)**, which controls the **number** of X-rays (quantity) and image **density**; and **exposure time**. Wilhelm Roentgen discovered X-rays in 1895, and C. Edmund Kells took the first dental radiograph in the United States.',
match('Match the factor to its effect',[['kVp','Penetrating power and contrast'],['mA','Quantity of X-rays and density'],['Exposure time','Duration of X-ray production'],['Aluminum filter','Removes low-energy rays'],['Rectangular collimator','Restricts the beam to reduce exposure'],['Lead apron with thyroid collar','Protects the patient’s body and thyroid']])]],
['Radiation safety',[
'Dental teams follow **ALARA**—as low as reasonably achievable. Radiographs are prescribed by the dentist based on the patient’s needs, following FDA/ADA patient selection guidelines, not on a fixed schedule. Patient protection includes **fast image receptors** (digital sensors or F-speed film), **rectangular collimation**, **thyroid collars** and lead or lead-equivalent aprons, correct technique to avoid retakes and proper processing. Operator protection: **never hold the receptor or tube head** for a patient, stand **behind a protective barrier** or at least **6 feet** from the tube head at a **90–135° angle** to the beam, and wear a **dosimeter badge** if required. Children are more sensitive to radiation, so imaging is sized to the child.',
chk('Where should the operator stand if no barrier is available?',['At least 6 feet from the tube head at a 90–135° angle to the beam','Directly behind the patient in the beam path','Holding the sensor in the patient’s mouth','Next to the tube head'],'Distance and position reduce scatter exposure.')]],
['Radiographic types, techniques and errors',[
'**Intraoral** images include **periapical** radiographs (the whole tooth and surrounding bone to the apex), **bitewing** radiographs (crowns of maxillary and mandibular teeth and the alveolar crest, ideal for **interproximal caries** and bone levels; the patient bites on a **bite-wing tab** or holder) and **occlusal** radiographs (large areas of the jaw). **Extraoral** images include the **panoramic** (both jaws in one image), **cephalometric** (skull profile for orthodontics) and **cone-beam computed tomography (CBCT)**, a 3-D image. The **paralleling technique**, in which the receptor is parallel to the long axis of the tooth and the beam perpendicular to both, is preferred; the **bisecting angle technique** is an alternative. Common errors include **elongation** (too little vertical angulation), **foreshortening** (too much vertical angulation), **overlapping** of contacts (incorrect horizontal angulation), **cone cut** (the PID not centered over the receptor), **blurring** (movement) and a **reversed receptor** (a herringbone or tire-track pattern on film). **Digital** radiography uses sensors or phosphor storage plates, reducing exposure and avoiding chemical processing; film is processed with developer, rinse, fixer, wash and drying. Receptors are covered with barriers for infection control.',
sort('Identify the error',['Vertical angulation error','Horizontal or centering error'],[['Teeth appear too long (elongation)',0],['Teeth appear too short (foreshortening)',0],['Overlapping proximal contacts',1],['Unexposed clear arc (cone cut)',1]])]],
['Reading a bitewing',[
'Radiographs show structures in shades of gray. **Radiolucent** areas appear **dark** because X-rays pass through them easily—air spaces, pulp, soft tissue and **caries**. **Radiopaque** areas appear **light or white** because they absorb X-rays—**enamel**, dentin (slightly less), bone and especially **metal restorations**. On a bitewing, interproximal caries appears as a dark triangle or notch in enamel and dentin near the contact, and the **alveolar crest** should lie about 1.5–2 mm below the CEJ in health.',
label('bitewing','Radiolucent or radiopaque on a bitewing',[['enamel','Enamel','Most radiopaque natural tissue.'],['dentin','Dentin','Radiopaque but less than enamel.'],['pulp','Pulp','Radiolucent chamber and canals.'],['restoration','Metal restoration','Very radiopaque (white).'],['caries','Caries','Radiolucent (dark) area of demineralization.'],['crest','Alveolar crest','Bone level checked for periodontal disease.'],['bone','Bone','Radiopaque with a trabecular pattern.']])]],
['Medical emergencies in the dental office',[
'Prevention begins with an **updated medical history** at each visit, **vital signs**, reducing patient anxiety and knowing each patient’s medications and allergies. Every office has an **emergency kit** (including **oxygen**, **epinephrine**, **nitroglycerin**, an **albuterol inhaler**, an **antihistamine**, **aspirin** and a **glucose** source), an **AED**, assigned team roles and regular drills; staff maintain **BLS/CPR** certification. **Syncope** (fainting) is the **most common** dental emergency, often from anxiety: the patient becomes pale, sweaty and lightheaded—stop treatment, place them **supine or subsupine with the legs elevated**, loosen tight clothing, give oxygen and monitor; an ammonia inhalant may be used per office protocol. **Postural hypotension** is prevented by raising the chair slowly. **Hyperventilation** (rapid breathing, tingling fingers, anxiety) is treated by calming the patient and coaching slow breathing—oxygen is not given. An **asthma attack** (wheezing) is treated with the patient upright and their **bronchodilator inhaler**.',
'**Angina** (chest pain) is treated with **nitroglycerin** sublingually and oxygen; pain that does not resolve suggests a **myocardial infarction**—call emergency services, give oxygen and aspirin as directed and be ready for CPR and the AED. **Allergic reactions** range from a rash (antihistamine) to **anaphylaxis** (hives, airway swelling, wheezing, falling blood pressure), which requires **epinephrine** and emergency services. **Hypoglycemia** (sweating, shakiness, confusion) is treated with fast-acting sugar if the patient is conscious. During a **seizure**, remove instruments and items from the mouth and area, protect the patient from injury, do not restrain or place anything in the mouth, and time it. **Stroke** signs (BE FAST) require emergency services immediately. **Airway obstruction** from a dropped crown or debris is prevented with throat packs and the HVE; a conscious choking adult receives **abdominal thrusts**.',
kase('An emergency in the chair',{a:{text:'During a local anesthetic injection, a nervous 22-year-old becomes pale, sweaty and says they feel faint. What is the first action?',choices:[['Stop, place the patient supine or subsupine with legs elevated, and give oxygen','b','Correct. These are signs of vasovagal syncope.',true],['Sit the patient upright quickly','b','Upright positioning reduces blood flow to the brain.',false]]},b:{text:'The patient recovers, but another patient later reports crushing chest pain that is not relieved after nitroglycerin. What next?',choices:[['Call emergency services, give oxygen and aspirin as directed and prepare the AED','c','Correct. Unrelieved chest pain suggests a heart attack.',true],['Reschedule the appointment and send them home','c','This is a medical emergency.',false]]},c:{text:'The team follows its assigned roles, documents the events and restocks the emergency kit afterward.',end:true}}),
flip('Emergency and first response',[['Syncope','Supine or subsupine, legs up, oxygen'],['Hyperventilation','Calm the patient, slow breathing; no oxygen'],['Asthma attack','Upright, patient’s bronchodilator'],['Angina','Nitroglycerin, oxygen; if unrelieved call 911'],['Anaphylaxis','Epinephrine, call 911, support airway'],['Hypoglycemia (conscious)','Fast-acting sugar']])]]
],takeaways:['kVp controls penetration and contrast; mA controls quantity and density; ALARA with fast receptors, rectangular collimation, thyroid collars; operator 6 ft at 90–135°.','Bitewings show interproximal caries and crest levels; paralleling is preferred; elongation/foreshortening are vertical errors, overlap horizontal, cone cut centering.','Syncope is most common: supine/subsupine, legs up, oxygen; angina: nitroglycerin; anaphylaxis: epinephrine; hyperventilation: slow breathing without oxygen.'],terms:[['Radiolucent','Appears dark because X-rays pass through.'],['Radiopaque','Appears light because X-rays are absorbed.'],['ALARA','As low as reasonably achievable radiation exposure.']],mcqs:[q('Which exposure factor controls the penetrating power of the X-ray beam?','kVp','mA','Exposure time','Film speed','It also affects contrast.'),q('Which structure appears radiolucent?','Pulp','Enamel','Amalgam restoration','Bone','Radiolucent areas are dark.'),q('Which radiograph is best for detecting interproximal caries?','Bitewing','Panoramic','Cephalometric','Occlusal','It shows crowns of both arches.'),q('Elongated images result from:','Too little vertical angulation','Too much vertical angulation','Incorrect horizontal angulation','Patient movement','Foreshortening is the opposite.'),q('The operator should stand at least how far from the tube head without a barrier?','6 feet','1 foot','2 feet','20 feet','At a 90–135° angle to the beam.'),q('What is the most common medical emergency in dental offices?','Syncope','Cardiac arrest','Stroke','Anaphylaxis','Often caused by anxiety.'),q('Which drug is used first for anaphylaxis?','Epinephrine','Nitroglycerin','Albuterol','Glucose','It reverses airway swelling and hypotension.'),q('How is hyperventilation managed?','Calm the patient and coach slow breathing','Give 100% oxygen','Give nitroglycerin','Place the patient in subsupine','Oxygen is not given for hyperventilation.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how a dental assistant protects the patient and themselves when exposing bitewing radiographs, how to evaluate the images, and how the office should respond if the patient faints during the appointment.',rubric:[['Patient protection',['alara','thyroid','apron','collimat','fast','digital','retake']],['Operator protection',['6 feet','barrier','90','135','never hold','dosimeter']],['Image evaluation',['interproximal','caries','radiolucent','radiopaque','overlap','cone cut','crest']],['Syncope response',['supine','subsupine','legs','oxygen','monitor']]],model:'Bitewings are taken only when the dentist prescribes them according to the patient’s needs, following ALARA. To protect the patient, I would use a fast digital sensor with a barrier, rectangular collimation, a lead apron with a thyroid collar and correct technique to avoid retakes. To protect myself, I would never hold the sensor or tube head and would stand behind a barrier or at least 6 feet away at a 90–135° angle to the beam. I would check that the images show the crowns of both arches with open contacts (no overlapping from horizontal angulation errors), no cone cuts and visible alveolar crests, then look for radiolucent (dark) interproximal caries and note radiopaque restorations and bone levels for the dentist. If the patient became pale, sweaty and faint, I would stop, remove items from the mouth, place the patient supine or subsupine with legs elevated, loosen tight clothing, give oxygen, monitor vital signs and call for help if they did not recover, then document the event.'},sources:[RULES,FDA_XRAY,IMAGE_GENTLY,AHA_CPR]};
